{"name":"Ice Ice Baby","hex":"#00ffdd","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2/B language code for Icelandic."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water in frozen (solid) form.","Any frozen volatile chemical, such as ammonia or carbon dioxide."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ice"},{"term":"baby","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very young human, particularly from birth to a couple of years old or until walking is fully mastered.","Any very young animal, especially a vertebrate; many species have specific names for their babies, such as kittens for the babies of cats, puppies for the babies of dogs, and chicks for the babies of birds. See Category:Baby animals for more."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Picked when small and immature (as in baby corn, baby potatoes).","Newest (overall, or in some group or state); most inexperienced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/baby"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ice Ice Baby","blocks":[{"text":"\"Ice Ice Baby\" is the debut single by American rapper Vanilla Ice. Released by SBK Records, on his debut album, To the Extreme (1990), it is his best-known song and has appeared in remixed form on Platinum Underground and Vanilla Ice Is Back! A live version appears on the album Extremely Live, while a nu metal version appears on the album Hard to Swallow, under the title \"Too Cold\". \"Ice Ice Baby\" samples the bassline of the song \"Under Pressure\" by British rock band Queen and British singer David Bowie, who did not receive songwriting credit or royalties until after it had become a hit."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3f/Vanilla_Ice_-_Ice_Ice_Baby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_Ice_Baby","wikidata":"Q2389029"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3f/Vanilla_Ice_-_Ice_Ice_Baby.jpg/250px-Vanilla_Ice_-_Ice_Ice_Baby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3f/Vanilla_Ice_-_Ice_Ice_Baby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3f/Vanilla_Ice_-_Ice_Ice_Baby.jpg/250px-Vanilla_Ice_-_Ice_Ice_Baby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVanilla_Ice_-_Ice_Ice_Baby.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Vanilla_Ice.jpg/330px-Vanilla_Ice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Vanilla_Ice.jpg/330px-Vanilla_Ice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vanilla Ice based the song's lyrics upon the South Florida area in which he lived.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVanilla_Ice.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_B